1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,880
This pH meter has just been calibrated, and

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:05,440
it reads distilled water as five point eight

3
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:06,080
two.

4
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:08,720
Shouldn't pure water be seven?

5
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:10,400
So is the meter faulty?

6
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:11,800
Hold that thought.

7
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:14,160
In this practical you measure the pH of

8
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:16,800
a range of acids and bases, and before

9
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:19,040
you trust a number like this, the method

10
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:20,000
has to be right.

11
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:25,680
Here's the range: hydrochloric acid, acetic acid, distilled

12
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:29,520
water, ammonia solution and sodium hydroxide.

13
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:32,240
The two acids and two bases are all

14
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:35,520
zero point zero one zero moles per litre,

15
00:00:35,640 --> 00:00:38,320
so any difference comes from the substance, not

16
00:00:38,360 --> 00:00:40,000
the amount dissolved.

17
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:42,480
Pour about twenty millilitres of each into a

18
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:44,080
clean, labelled beaker.

19
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:46,940
How does the meter know?

20
00:00:47,220 --> 00:00:49,300
The probe has a thin glass bulb at

21
00:00:49,380 --> 00:00:49,860
its tip.

22
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:53,220
Hydronium ions in the solution produce a tiny

23
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:56,140
voltage across that glass, and the meter turns

24
00:00:56,180 --> 00:00:58,020
the voltage into a pH number.

25
00:00:58,660 --> 00:01:01,460
But the link between voltage and pH drifts

26
00:01:01,540 --> 00:01:03,860
as the probe ages, and it changes with

27
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:04,500
temperature.

28
00:01:05,060 --> 00:01:06,740
So the meter has to be shown what

29
00:01:06,780 --> 00:01:08,340
the right answers look like.

30
00:01:09,567 --> 00:01:10,846
That's calibration.

31
00:01:11,247 --> 00:01:13,566
You dip the probe into buffer solutions, which

32
00:01:13,647 --> 00:01:15,646
hold a known, steady pH.

33
00:01:16,207 --> 00:01:18,926
Start with the pH seven buffer, then pH

34
00:01:19,007 --> 00:01:21,726
four, and add pH ten because you'll measure

35
00:01:21,967 --> 00:01:22,686
bases too.

36
00:01:23,327 --> 00:01:25,406
The meter draws a straight line through those

37
00:01:25,487 --> 00:01:26,566
known points.

38
00:01:26,847 --> 00:01:29,166
One buffer gives only one point, so the

39
00:01:29,247 --> 00:01:30,926
meter would have to guess the slope.

40
00:01:31,567 --> 00:01:33,646
Buffers on both sides of seven check the

41
00:01:33,727 --> 00:01:35,726
line for acids and for bases.

42
00:01:36,367 --> 00:01:39,006
Calibrate at the same temperature as your samples,

43
00:01:39,087 --> 00:01:40,286
on the day you measure.

44
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:44,700
Then, for every sample: rinse the probe with

45
00:01:44,780 --> 00:01:47,580
distilled water, and gently blot it.

46
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:49,580
Any solution left on the glass would be

47
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:51,820
carried into the next beaker, and change its

48
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:52,940
reading.

49
00:01:53,020 --> 00:01:56,140
Lower the bulb fully in, swirl gently, and

50
00:01:56,220 --> 00:01:58,700
wait until the reading stops changing, because the

51
00:01:58,780 --> 00:02:01,020
glass needs time to respond.

52
00:02:01,180 --> 00:02:04,060
Take three readings, each in a fresh portion,

53
00:02:04,180 --> 00:02:07,340
so one contaminated portion can't affect all three.

54
00:02:08,567 --> 00:02:11,126
Here are one class's results, as means of

55
00:02:11,207 --> 00:02:12,366
three readings.

56
00:02:12,647 --> 00:02:15,686
Hydrochloric acid, two point zero three.

57
00:02:15,807 --> 00:02:18,566
Acetic acid, three point four zero.

58
00:02:18,687 --> 00:02:22,086
Distilled water, five point nine six, with readings

59
00:02:22,167 --> 00:02:23,206
that wandered.

60
00:02:23,287 --> 00:02:25,846
Ammonia, ten point five nine.

61
00:02:25,967 --> 00:02:29,686
And sodium hydroxide, eleven point nine six.

62
00:02:29,847 --> 00:02:32,086
Notice the repeats for each acid and base

63
00:02:32,167 --> 00:02:34,966
agree within about zero point zero two.

64
00:02:36,167 --> 00:02:37,766
So what do the numbers say about the

65
00:02:37,847 --> 00:02:38,646
ions?

66
00:02:38,887 --> 00:02:42,246
Below seven, a solution has more hydronium ions

67
00:02:42,327 --> 00:02:43,926
than hydroxide ions.

68
00:02:44,167 --> 00:02:46,486
Above seven, more hydroxide.

69
00:02:46,727 --> 00:02:49,766
Hydrochloric acid reads about one point four lower

70
00:02:49,847 --> 00:02:50,886
than acetic acid.

71
00:02:51,287 --> 00:02:54,246
Each pH step is ten times, so two

72
00:02:54,327 --> 00:02:56,566
steps would be a hundred, and this is

73
00:02:56,647 --> 00:02:59,446
in between: about twenty three times as many

74
00:02:59,527 --> 00:03:02,486
hydronium ions, at the same concentration.

75
00:03:02,727 --> 00:03:05,606
That's strength, and the next practical tests it

76
00:03:05,687 --> 00:03:06,246
properly.

77
00:03:06,647 --> 00:03:10,486
Both bases have extra hydroxide, sodium hydroxide far

78
00:03:10,567 --> 00:03:11,126
more.

79
00:03:12,334 --> 00:03:13,213
Now the water.

80
00:03:13,294 --> 00:03:15,133
The meter wasn't faulty.

81
00:03:15,614 --> 00:03:18,253
Carbon dioxide from the air dissolves in water

82
00:03:18,414 --> 00:03:20,973
and forms a little carbonic acid, which gives

83
00:03:21,054 --> 00:03:23,213
a few hydronium ions to the water.

84
00:03:23,774 --> 00:03:26,173
So water left open to the air really

85
00:03:26,254 --> 00:03:27,533
is slightly acidic.

86
00:03:28,014 --> 00:03:30,653
And with so few ions, the probe struggles

87
00:03:30,734 --> 00:03:33,613
to settle, so the readings drift and spread.

88
00:03:34,174 --> 00:03:36,653
Distilled doesn't always mean neutral.

89
00:03:37,867 --> 00:03:39,306
Why use a meter at all?

90
00:03:39,947 --> 00:03:42,746
Universal indicator paper gives a colour, which you

91
00:03:42,907 --> 00:03:44,746
match to a chart to about the nearest

92
00:03:44,827 --> 00:03:47,306
whole number, and two people can judge the

93
00:03:47,467 --> 00:03:49,226
same colour differently.

94
00:03:49,307 --> 00:03:51,946
The meter gives two decimal places.

95
00:03:52,027 --> 00:03:55,066
But careful: those decimals only mean something if

96
00:03:55,107 --> 00:03:56,666
the meter is calibrated.

97
00:03:57,867 --> 00:03:59,306
So, the variables.

98
00:03:59,387 --> 00:04:03,066
The independent variable is which solution you test.

99
00:04:03,547 --> 00:04:06,026
The dependent variable is the pH.

100
00:04:06,267 --> 00:04:09,066
And the controlled variables are the concentration, the

101
00:04:09,147 --> 00:04:12,026
temperature, the volume in each beaker, the same

102
00:04:12,107 --> 00:04:15,066
calibrated meter, and the same rinsing routine.

103
00:04:16,267 --> 00:04:19,226
Now the three words from the equilibrium practical.

104
00:04:19,547 --> 00:04:22,146
Validity: are you measuring the pH of the

105
00:04:22,187 --> 00:04:23,386
solution you meant to?

106
00:04:24,107 --> 00:04:27,946
Rinsing, fresh portions and labelled beakers keep anything

107
00:04:28,107 --> 00:04:29,586
else out of the reading.

108
00:04:30,027 --> 00:04:33,306
Reliability: do repeated readings agree?

109
00:04:33,387 --> 00:04:35,546
For the acids and bases, yes.

110
00:04:35,627 --> 00:04:37,626
For the water, no.

111
00:04:38,267 --> 00:04:40,346
Accuracy: how close is the reading to the

112
00:04:40,587 --> 00:04:41,706
true pH?

113
00:04:41,867 --> 00:04:44,866
That depends on calibration: buffers on both sides

114
00:04:44,907 --> 00:04:47,026
of seven, as close to your readings as

115
00:04:47,067 --> 00:04:49,386
you can, at your samples' temperature.

116
00:04:50,600 --> 00:04:51,800
The risk assessment.

117
00:04:52,440 --> 00:04:55,080
At zero point zero one zero moles per

118
00:04:55,160 --> 00:04:58,280
litre, these acids and bases are too dilute

119
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:01,160
to be classified as hazardous, but wear safety

120
00:05:01,240 --> 00:05:03,480
glasses anyway, because a splash in the eye

121
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:05,320
can still irritate.

122
00:05:05,360 --> 00:05:07,480
They're made up from stronger stocks, and the

123
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:09,960
concentrated ones are corrosive.

124
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:12,920
The glass bulb is thin and breaks easily,

125
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:15,160
so keep the probe in its holder, and

126
00:05:15,320 --> 00:05:16,280
never stir with it.

127
00:05:17,500 --> 00:05:21,180
Here's the routine to remember: calibrate first, rinse

128
00:05:21,260 --> 00:05:23,340
between, wait for steady.

129
00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:25,900
And when you read the result, one pH

130
00:05:25,980 --> 00:05:28,460
step is ten times the hydronium.

131
00:05:29,667 --> 00:05:30,306
Your turn.

132
00:05:30,867 --> 00:05:34,866
A student measures sodium hydroxide solution, then, without

133
00:05:34,907 --> 00:05:37,986
rinsing the probe, measures distilled water and gets

134
00:05:38,067 --> 00:05:39,666
nine point two.

135
00:05:39,747 --> 00:05:41,666
Other groups got about six for the same

136
00:05:41,747 --> 00:05:42,706
water.

137
00:05:42,827 --> 00:05:45,266
Explain the difference, and describe how she should

138
00:05:45,347 --> 00:05:46,706
change her method.

139
00:05:46,787 --> 00:05:48,146
Pause here to answer.

140
00:05:58,567 --> 00:06:01,206
Sodium hydroxide left on the probe was carried

141
00:06:01,287 --> 00:06:02,086
into the water.

142
00:06:02,727 --> 00:06:05,206
Water has almost no ions of its own,

143
00:06:05,287 --> 00:06:07,926
so a tiny amount of hydroxide raises its

144
00:06:08,007 --> 00:06:09,366
pH a lot.

145
00:06:09,687 --> 00:06:11,686
She should rinse and blot the probe between

146
00:06:11,767 --> 00:06:14,166
samples, then measure a fresh portion.

147
00:06:14,727 --> 00:06:18,166
Averaging more readings wouldn't fix it: without rinsing,

148
00:06:18,247 --> 00:06:21,366
the carried-over hydroxide still pushes the average up.

149
00:06:22,567 --> 00:06:23,206
One more.

150
00:06:23,687 --> 00:06:26,326
A student calibrates with a pH seven buffer

151
00:06:26,487 --> 00:06:30,326
only, then reads the hydrochloric acid three times:

152
00:06:30,407 --> 00:06:32,806
two point three one, two point three zero

153
00:06:33,207 --> 00:06:34,566
and two point three two.

154
00:06:35,207 --> 00:06:38,566
Another group, using pH seven and four buffers,

155
00:06:38,687 --> 00:06:40,246
gets two point zero three.

156
00:06:40,887 --> 00:06:44,246
Evaluate the reliability and accuracy of her results.

157
00:06:44,807 --> 00:06:46,086
Pause here to answer.

158
00:06:56,500 --> 00:06:59,460
Her results are reliable: the three readings agree

159
00:06:59,620 --> 00:07:01,460
within zero point zero two.

160
00:07:01,940 --> 00:07:04,420
But they aren't accurate: they sit about zero

161
00:07:04,580 --> 00:07:06,740
point three above the other group's two point

162
00:07:06,820 --> 00:07:07,860
zero three.

163
00:07:08,180 --> 00:07:10,260
With one buffer, the meter had to guess

164
00:07:10,380 --> 00:07:12,900
its slope, so every reading shares the same

165
00:07:13,060 --> 00:07:13,700
error.

166
00:07:14,020 --> 00:07:16,980
Calibrate with pH four as well, then repeat.

167
00:07:17,300 --> 00:07:20,340
"They agree, so they're accurate" is the classic

168
00:07:20,420 --> 00:07:20,900
slip.

169
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:25,140
So: calibrate with buffers on both sides of

170
00:07:25,220 --> 00:07:28,580
seven, rinse and blot between samples, wait for

171
00:07:28,660 --> 00:07:31,940
a steady reading, and repeat in fresh portions.

172
00:07:32,260 --> 00:07:36,180
Then read each number as hydronium against hydroxide,

173
00:07:36,300 --> 00:07:37,860
each step ten times.

174
00:07:39,060 --> 00:07:42,020
Next: using pH to compare strong and weak

175
00:07:42,100 --> 00:07:42,500
acids and

176
00:07:43,060 --> 00:07:43,300
bases.

177
00:07:44,500 --> 00:07:45,940
Here are the key notes.

178
00:07:46,180 --> 00:07:48,100
Pause the video if you need more time

179
00:07:48,180 --> 00:07:48,980
to copy them.
